A||N||7||86SEv6||Lack of power||||9||
Z||000000||86SEv6||05-10-2001||09:23 AM||||My 86SEv6 all of a sudden lacks it's power and acceleration! Even in Neutral and Park-so it's confined to my engine only (not tranny). Idles fine in N or P; but, drops from like 1000 rpm to 500 then eventually stalls in Drive. It's been only 3400 mi since a complete head gasket job including complete tune-up (new cap, rotor, plugs & wires). Max speed is 25 mph. Doesn't overheat. Checked timing and was off the scale: my guess would be 40deg btdc(3400 mi ago it was 10deg btdc). My timing mark's always been jumpy. what bothers me is that the #4 timing is about several degrees more retarded than #1--they matched when I tuned it up 3400 mi ago! No trouble codes. Got fuel and spark. What could it be?! Vacuum lines are fine. Somebody help, please!||||reg||9
Z||000001||StuGood||05-10-2001||07:56 PM||||The timing change should have occurred last month (Spring ahead  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?).  My first thought would be that the timing chain might have skipped a notch or two.  Or, maybe the distributor was not installed all the way in its hole?<P>First mark the distributor position, so you can go back to where you found it.  Next get a timing light, connect ALDL terminals A & B, and set the timing where it's supposed to be - 10 degrees BTDC.  If the timing shifted that much, and if the distributor was installed correctly and isn't damaged, it may be timing chain time!   ||||reg||3
Z||000002||Mark||05-10-2001||10:02 PM||||I assume you shorted the pins when you set and checked the timing?  I agree that it could be the timing chain.  Once upon a time, I jumped a tooth on the timing belt in my VW GTI 16V.  Afterward, the car couldn't get out of its own way.  The timing was way off when I checked it.  Funny thing was, the timing belt was relatively new, and correctly installed.<p>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||86SEv6||05-10-2001||10:15 PM||||Stugood, <BR>thanks very much for your quick reply. I reset the timing as you said to 10deg btdc, again. My idle in drive is around 500rpm in Drive and wants to die. It likes 40deg btdc the best for some reason. Before replacing the timing chain and sprockets there are some things I want to rule out: exhaust pipe/cat conv restriction( i will disconnect them); lack of fuel pressure(i will measure it and compare to manual); and finally, the ignition module(i'll have autozone test it & may substitute it with a new one). If after all those adjustments no improvement to power occurs, I will blame my problem on either a worn distributor or timing chain/gear problem. What do you think? I'll keep y'all updated. THANX!<P><p>[This message has been edited by 86SEv6 (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000004||87GTZ34||05-10-2001||10:18 PM||||86SEV6 - You are describing my car's symptoms to the tee.  I recently bought a 2nd GT from its original owner.  He said it was driven daily.  When I got it home and started diggin in, I know that car could not be driven daily as it was in sorry shape.  I did the top end including a full head rebuild.  My timing is jumpy, the car stumbles/fumbles with no or little load on it.  If I put some load on it (accelerate somewhat hard) it doesn't miss a beat. It is not a periodic miss but an inconsistent stumbling.  When I did the heads I did notice the timing chain had lots 'o slop.  I didn't have parts at that time so I figured I would drive it awhile to see what else cropped up.  Sure enough the front crank seal is leaking so there are two reasons to pull the cover.  Anyway, I am quite sure my problem is the timing chain.  The eng has good compression, the dist was replaced not too long ago and is in excellent shape, I do need to check fuel pressure but I think I would have a problem under load if I had low pressure. I haven't checked my timing lately but I will keep you posted as to what I find.  I also need to replace my TPS as the housing is cracked on it.  I don't think that is the stumbling problem either.  It acts like variable valve timing (uncontrolled though).  It may get enough air/fuel and the chain bounces around and cuts short a few cylinders and then recovers.||||reg||3
Z||000005||86SEv6||05-10-2001||10:23 PM||||Yes Mark,<BR>I did ground A & B of my ALDL. Before doing that my emmissions sticker told me to block my wheels, set my parking brake, and then put it in drive. I set timing after it had warmed up; but, before the cooling fan turns on.||||reg||1
Z||000006||86SEv6||05-10-2001||10:32 PM||||87GTZ34,<BR>It's comforting to know that i'm not the only one out there with this exact problem. Your's does sound like my car's missing twin; except that my crank seal is doing it's job(thank God!). My distribitor may be suspect cause I don't know how new it is. I did replace my TPS as you plan on doing; but, it didn't help any with me. Well let's hope we can both eventually fix our guys. I'll keep you posted and hope you do the same. Thanx!<p>[This message has been edited by 86SEv6 (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||05-11-2001||09:03 PM||||Update on my situation...  I replaced the TPS (mine was actually cracked and open).  It made no difference and I didn't think it would.  I changed it so if I'm driving in the rain, I won't get stranded.  Alex has offered to use his autoxray so I will do that.  I don't expect to find anything out of the ordinary.  Vacuum is good (19 in), fuel system 'seems' to be working as designed.  My gut tells me the slop in the timing chain is in fact the culprit.  My parts haven't arrived yet so I won't find out until next WE.  Other symptoms my car has - the fwd trans mount was melted (i believe from an overheated cat).  The cat no longer is converting as there is no sulfer smell, the exhaust smells like the car doesn't have a cat. Rebuilt heads full tuneup, timing was on when I set it but need to re-check it.||||reg||1
